<Project>

	<!-- Repository-wide security gate. Turns "dotnet restore" and "dotnet build" into
	     Validation Gate 1 (static analysis) and Gate 2 (dependency analysis), so a
	     reintroduced vulnerability fails the build rather than needing to be noticed.
	     Findings, evidence and disposition: docs/security/security-audit-report.md,
	     docs/security/remediation-log.md, docs/security/risk-register.md.

	     MSBuild rather than a repository-root .editorconfig: all four .editorconfig
	     files here declare "root = true", so a root style file would be ignored inside
	     the four subtrees that hold the core, web and plugin libraries. MSBuild imports
	     this file for every project beneath this directory regardless of that scoping,
	     so it reaches all 19 projects whether the build is driven from the solution or
	     from one project file.

	     This is the whole build-level gate: no companion analyzer configuration file
	     exists in the tree and none may be added (see the prohibition below). The SDK
	     is pinned in global.json; properties that already match an SDK default are
	     stated anyway so the gate is defined here rather than by a default that moves
	     between toolchains.

	     Deliberately absent, and not to be added: target framework, language version,
	     nullable or implicit-usings settings, package metadata, package references,
	     central package version management, a lock file, and a companion .targets
	     import. Each project owns its own compilation and packaging settings. -->

	<!-- THREAT ADDRESSED: use of components with known vulnerabilities (OWASP A06:2021,
	     CWE-1104, CWE-1035). Serves Gate 2 by auditing the advisory database on every
	     restore. Mode "all" is load-bearing rather than belt-and-braces: this graph has
	     carried a transitive-only advisory (MimeKit, reached through MailKit), which
	     "direct" never reports. Level "low" stops a Moderate advisory hiding beneath a
	     High-only bar. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
		<NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
		<NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- THREAT ADDRESSED: an advisory that is reported but ignored. An audit that only
	     warns is not a gate. NU1901 to NU1904 are the low/moderate/high/critical audit
	     diagnostics; NU1900 and NU1905 are the data-availability ones, and including
	     them is the load-bearing part, because an audit that cannot reach an advisory
	     database otherwise reports nothing and the build goes green on a vulnerable
	     package. Appended, never assigned: the SDK contributes NU1605 and SYSLIB0011 to
	     this property and a bare literal would drop both.

	     By design, an advisory anywhere in the graph fails restore and build. Clear it
	     by upgrading the package, or accept it through a recorded risk acceptance in
	     docs/security/risk-register.md - never by deleting this property group.

	     RESIDUAL, closed elsewhere: a source that resolves the package but serves no
	     advisory data at all emits no diagnostic for this group to promote. The
	     workflow's advisory negative control covers that case by restoring a knowingly
	     vulnerable probe and failing when the restore SUCCEEDS. Neither mechanism is
	     redundant with the other. A transient outage therefore fails the build instead
	     of passing quietly; re-run the job rather than demoting these two codes. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1900;NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904;NU1905</WarningsAsErrors>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- THREAT ADDRESSED: insecure coding patterns reaching production unreviewed
	     (OWASP A02:2021, A03:2021, A08:2021). Serves Gate 1. DISCLOSED SUBSTITUTION:
	     the external SAST tooling named in the audit brief could not be installed here,
	     so the SDK's Roslyn analyzers stand in for it, which also keeps the gate free
	     of any new package reference.

	     "latest-recommended" arms CA5350 and CA5351 (weak and broken hashing), CA5359
	     (disabled certificate validation) and CA5364 (deprecated transport protocols).

	     AnalysisLevelSecurity is what arms the remaining security families, and the
	     LEVEL form is the only form that works: the SDK target that selects the
	     Security category's configuration reads the level suffix first, and because
	     AnalysisLevelSecurity defaults to AnalysisLevel the "recommended" suffix wins
	     and AnalysisModeSecurity is never consulted. Do not "simplify" this to the mode
	     property. Setting the level to "all" selects the SDK's own shipped
	     configuration for the category, which is why the widening needs no file in this
	     tree. It arms query construction (CA2100), unrestricted type-name handling
	     (CA2326 to CA2328), hard-coded keys (CA5390), non-random initialisation vectors
	     (CA5401) and disabled token-validation checks (CA5404).

	     NOTHING IS PROMOTED TO AN ERROR, and nothing may be: the WarningsAsErrors
	     promotion above carries NU19xx codes and no CA identifier. Arming a family and
	     escalating it are different acts; escalating a roughly 700-file legacy backlog
	     would force the repository-wide refactor the remediation scope forbids. The
	     analyzer diagnostics therefore stay warnings, which is why the workflow
	     measures them instead of reading the build's exit code, and why its pass
	     criterion is absent-or-justified rather than zero: it enumerates each (rule,
	     file) pair, requires a written justification, and fails on any unreviewed pair
	     or any increase in count. Some armed rules fire on the remediation's own
	     sanctioned mitigations - CA2326 on the presence of type-name handling even
	     where the allow-listing binder is attached, CA5351 on the legacy digest
	     verification path that exists so stored credentials keep working - so silence
	     is never accepted as proof and deleting them would undo the remediation. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
		<AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
		<AnalysisLevelSecurity>latest-all</AnalysisLevelSecurity>
		<!-- The taint family and the one compilation it is excluded from are named as
		     properties so the workflow's Gate 1 reads the boundary out of this file
		     instead of carrying its own copy; two copies of a security boundary drift.
		     Edit them here rather than from the command line, because MSBuild splits a
		     command-line property value on ';'. -->
		<ErpTaintAnalysisFamily>CA3001;CA3002;CA3003;CA3004;CA3005;CA3006;CA3007;CA3008;CA3009;CA3010;CA3011;CA3012</ErpTaintAnalysisFamily>
		<ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject>WebVella.Erp.Web</ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- THE ONE EXCLUSION, AND IT IS A TERMINATION CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
	     The interprocedural taint family is superlinear in compilation size, and
	     WebVella.Erp.Web compiles 395 Razor views into a single compilation: armed and
	     unbounded it does not terminate, while each of the other 18 projects completes
	     in seconds and reports zero. So the exclusion is conditioned on that one
	     project name and the family stays armed everywhere else. Do not widen it to
	     another CA identifier or another project without re-measuring per project.

	     The excluded compilation is still scanned - separately and bounded - by the
	     workflow step that arms the family for it with a cost bound, because an
	     unscanned compilation and a clean one are indistinguishable from outside. What
	     remains residual there is DEPTH, not coverage, and is carried as RISK-051.

	     Set ErpSecurityTaintAnalysis=all to arm the family everywhere, which is how the
	     exclusion is re-measured on a future toolchain without editing this file. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ErpSecurityTaintAnalysis)' != 'all' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == '$(ErpTaintAnalysisExcludedProject)'">
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);$(ErpTaintAnalysisFamily)</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- The hook that carries the cost bound for that separate scan. A dataflow cost
	     bound can only be expressed as analyzer configuration, so this lets a caller
	     supply ONE such file by absolute path, written outside the repository and never
	     tracked. Inert unless ErpTaintAnalysisOptions is set, so every ordinary
	     restore, build, publish, pack and IDE load compiles exactly as measured. -->
	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(ErpTaintAnalysisOptions)' != ''">
		<GlobalAnalyzerConfigFiles Include="$(ErpTaintAnalysisOptions)" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- NO GLOBAL ANALYZER CONFIG IS SUPPLIED, AND NONE MAY BE ADDED. A '.globalconfig'
	     at the repository root is the one mechanism that can change any rule's SEVERITY
	     for all 19 projects at once, because the SDK discovers it by walking every
	     directory above each project rather than by .editorconfig root scoping. Per-rule
	     escalation is outside this gate's scope, so no such file and no EditorConfigFiles
	     item may introduce one. Gate 1 asserts this absence over the tree, tracked or
	     untracked, and separately asserts which configuration the compiler loaded. -->

	<!-- Machine-readable analyzer evidence, opt-in. Supplying a directory makes each
	     project emit its own SARIF 2.1 log, so a verdict can be diffed or filtered
	     instead of re-derived from build text. Operator-invoked rather than wired into
	     CI, because the raw output is megabytes of the pre-existing backlog that would
	     bury the security signal; the workflow publishes Gate 1's derived evidence
	     instead. The path is per project: one shared ErrorLog would have the projects
	     overwrite one another and the surviving report would describe only whichever
	     finished last. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)' != ''">
		<ErrorLog>$(SecurityAnalyzerSarifDirectory)/$(MSBuildProjectName).sarif,version=2.1</ErrorLog>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- DOC-COMMENT DIAGNOSTICS, OPT-IN. Review finding N1, gate half. THREAT ADDRESSED:
	     a gate blindness rather than a code weakness. The compiler only PARSES XML
	     documentation comments when it is asked to emit a documentation file, so
	     CS1570 (malformed XML in a doc comment), CS1571 (a parameter documented twice)
	     and CS1573 (a parameter left undocumented while its siblings are documented)
	     are invisible to every ordinary build and to every step of the workflow.
	     Eleven CS1570 defects consequently reached the tracked tree and were caught by
	     human review instead of by CI. Setting this property is what lets the analyzer
	     build measure those rules alongside the CA families, so the same class of
	     regression is caught mechanically next time.

	     IT IS OPT-IN RATHER THAN ALWAYS-ON because generating documentation also arms
	     CS1591, missing comment on a publicly visible member, which this repository
	     carries in the thousands as a pre-existing backlog. Left to speak it would bury
	     the security signal in the evidence Gate 1 publishes, and clearing it would be
	     the mass documentation pass the engagement's minimal-change clause forbids. So
	     CS1591 is silenced here, under this condition and nowhere else, which keeps
	     every ordinary restore, build, publish, pack and IDE load compiling exactly as
	     measured.

	     WHY THE SUPPRESSION IS WRITTEN IN THIS FILE AND MUST NEVER MOVE TO A COMMAND
	     LINE. A `-p:NoWarn=CS1591` on the invocation becomes a GLOBAL MSBuild property,
	     and a global property OVERRIDES rather than appends - so the conditioned NoWarn
	     above stops contributing and the CA3001-CA3012 taint exclusion silently
	     disarms. Measured, not theorised: a documentation build invoked that way armed
	     the unbounded interprocedural taint family against WebVella.Erp.Web and had to
	     be abandoned after 21 minutes. Appending inside a PropertyGroup preserves
	     $(NoWarn), so the exclusion survives; this group is placed after it for exactly
	     that reason. No CA identifier is added to NoWarn here, so Gate 1's NoWarn
	     boundary assertion - which rejects any CA code outside the measured taint
	     exclusion - is unaffected, and that step evaluates the property without this
	     opt-in in any case. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(ErpDocumentationDiagnostics)' == 'true'">
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- LICENCE GOVERNANCE GATE. THREAT ADDRESSED: a supply-chain integrity one rather
	     than a code weakness. Four manifests here declare PackageLicenseExpression
	     Apache-2.0 and are published to nuget.org - WebVella.Erp, WebVella.Erp.Web,
	     WebVella.Erp.Plugins.Mail and WebVella.Erp.Plugins.SDK - and all four sit on a
	     graph reaching AutoMapper, which from 15.1.1 onward is published under the
	     Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing an Apache-2.0 claim over that graph
	     states a licence the package does not carry, to every downstream consumer,
	     IRREVOCABLY. Only the repository owner may settle it, so the unresolved
	     decision is made mechanically unshippable instead. See RISK-001.

	     It lives in this file, not in the core manifest, so all four packable projects
	     are covered by one definition; placing it in one manifest left the other three
	     packable with the same unratified expression and no diagnostic.

	     It gates `dotnet pack` ONLY, because packing is the only action that publishes
	     the claim. restore, build, publish, run, `dotnet list package` and every CI
	     gate step are unaffected.

	     THE ANSWER MUST BE A COMMITTED RECORD, NOT A PROPERTY. A global property lives
	     for one process: it records no approver, no date and no reviewable trace, and
	     any script can supply it. ERPLIC005 therefore refuses that form by name rather
	     than ignoring it. The gate reads its answer from docs/security/risk-register.md,
	     beside RISK-001, as exactly one line with no semicolons:
	         AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: <answer> | approver: <name and contact>
	         | date: <YYYY-MM-DD> | ref: <ticket or URL>
	     where <answer> is accepted-rpl-1.5 or declined-rpl-1.5. Anything else is
	     refused rather than ignored, so a typo cannot read as consent. The register
	     documents this shape with a distinct example token, so neither the example nor
	     any prose mention can satisfy the patterns below.

	     The conflict is DETECTED, not hard-coded, so the gate disables itself once it
	     stops applying: the pin is read from the core manifest at pack time and
	     compared against ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions. If AutoMapper publishes a
	     patched permissively licensed release and the pin moves to it, add that version
	     to the list as a reviewed edit and packing proceeds with no property at all.
	     Scope errs towards over-inclusion - any project declaring a licence expression
	     is covered - because over-inclusion costs an authorisation the owner owes
	     anyway while under-inclusion costs an irrevocable publication. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<ErpCoreProjectFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)WebVella.Erp/WebVella.Erp.csproj</ErpCoreProjectFile>
		<!-- Every AutoMapper release published under a permissive expression, in both
		     bracketed and bare notation. Extend only after reading the release's own
		     .nuspec, and edit here rather than from the command line (';' splitting). -->
		<ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions Condition="'$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions)' == ''">[14.0.0];14.0.0</ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions>
		<ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)docs/security/risk-register.md</ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<Target Name="ErpAssertAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded"
	        BeforeTargets="GenerateNuspec"
	        Condition="'$(PackageLicenseExpression)' != ''">
		<!-- The pin is read from the single manifest that declares it, so every packable
		     project is judged against the same fact rather than its own view of the
		     graph. The core manifest has no XML namespace, so a plain XPath applies. -->
		<XmlPeek XmlInputPath="$(ErpCoreProjectFile)"
		         Query="/Project/ItemGroup/PackageReference[@Include='AutoMapper']/@Version"
		         Condition="Exists('$(ErpCoreProjectFile)')">
			<Output TaskParameter="Result" ItemName="ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem" />
		</XmlPeek>
		<PropertyGroup>
			<ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>@(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersionItem)</ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>false</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != '' AND !$(ErpPermissiveAutoMapperVersions.Contains('$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)'))">true</ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The record is read inside the target, so an ordinary build never opens the
		     file and only `dotnet pack` pays for it. The text defaults to a non-empty
		     sentinel because a property function invoked with an empty argument is a
		     syntax error rather than a false, so an absent record file would break the
		     gate instead of blocking it. The anchor requires a decision-shaped token
		     FOLLOWED BY A PIPE, which is what keeps a prose mention harmless while a
		     malformed real attempt is still recognised and refused by name. -->
		<PropertyGroup>
			<ErpLicenceRecordText>(no licence decision record file)</ErpLicenceRecordText>
			<ErpLicenceRecordText Condition="Exists('$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)')">$([System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)))</ErpLicenceRecordText>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAnchor>AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION:[ \t]*[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+[ \t]*\|</ErpLicenceRecordAnchor>
			<ErpLicenceRecordShape>AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION:[ \t]*DECISIONTOKEN[ \t]*\|[ \t]*approver:[ \t]*[^|]+\|[ \t]*date:[ \t]*[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}[ \t]*\|[ \t]*ref:[ \t]*[^ \t|]+</ErpLicenceRecordShape>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAttempted>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordAnchor)))</ErpLicenceRecordAttempted>
			<ErpLicenceRecordAccepted>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordShape.Replace('DECISIONTOKEN','accepted-rpl-1\.5'))))</ErpLicenceRecordAccepted>
			<ErpLicenceRecordDeclined>$([System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex]::IsMatch($(ErpLicenceRecordText), $(ErpLicenceRecordShape.Replace('DECISIONTOKEN','declined-rpl-1\.5'))))</ErpLicenceRecordDeclined>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' == 'true'">accepted-rpl-1.5</ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded>
			<ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordDeclined)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' != 'true'">declined-rpl-1.5</ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded>
		</PropertyGroup>
		<!-- Every branch below fails closed: an unreadable pin, an unreadable record, a
		     contradiction and a malformed answer each block packing rather than pass,
		     because a gate that cannot read its input must not consent on the owner's
		     behalf. -->
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC004"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the AutoMapper pin could not be read from '$(ErpCoreProjectFile)', so the licence-governance gate cannot evaluate whether this package's declared PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' is accurate. This fails closed on purpose - a gate that cannot read its input must not pass. Restore the manifest, or update ErpCoreProjectFile in Directory.Build.props. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision)' != ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC005"
		       Text="ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecision was supplied as an MSBuild property, and that form is no longer accepted as approval (a transient 'dotnet pack -p:...' property is not durable authenticated approval - it records no approver, no date and no reviewable trace, and any script can supply it). Record the decision instead as one committed line in docs/security/risk-register.md: 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL'. Then pack with no property at all. See RISK-001 for both options and the reversal steps." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND !Exists('$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)')"
		       Code="ERPLIC006"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the licence decision record file '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' does not exist, so the owner's licence decision cannot be read and this gate cannot evaluate whether $(MSBuildProjectName)'s declared PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' is accurate. This fails closed on purpose - a gate that cannot read its input must not pass. Restore the file, or update ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile in Directory.Build.props." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAccepted)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpLicenceRecordDeclined)' == 'true'"
		       Code="ERPLIC007"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' contains BOTH an accepted-rpl-1.5 and a declined-rpl-1.5 decision record. Two contradictory answers are not resolved by order or precedence here, because either choice would be this gate inventing the owner's intent. Leave exactly one record line and remove the other. See RISK-001." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpLicenceRecordAttempted)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC003"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)' contains something shaped like an AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION record, but it does not match the required form, so it is refused rather than ignored - a typo must not be mistaken for the owner's consent. The line must read exactly: 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL', with declined-rpl-1.5 as the only other accepted answer, an ISO date, and no semicolons. See RISK-001." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == ''"
		       Code="ERPLIC001"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: $(MSBuildProjectName) declares PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' while its dependency graph reaches AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion), which is published under the Reciprocal Public License 1.5. Publishing that combination would state a licence this package does not carry, to every downstream consumer, irrevocably. This is a repository-owner decision (RISK-001) and is deliberately not settled automatically. To pack, commit ONE line to docs/security/risk-register.md - 'AUTOMAPPER-LICENCE-DECISION: accepted-rpl-1.5 | approver: NAME AND CONTACT | date: YYYY-MM-DD | ref: TICKET OR URL' - so the approval is durable, attributable and reviewable; or decline it, revert the pin using the reversal steps in RISK-001, and pack normally. Build, publish and run are unaffected by this gate." />
		<Error Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == 'declined-rpl-1.5'"
		       Code="ERPLIC002"
		       Text="Packing is blocked: the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 is recorded as DECLINED in '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)', but AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion) is still referenced, so $(MSBuildProjectName)'s declared licence expression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' would still be inaccurate. Complete the decline by reverting the pin as documented in docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001), which also restores advisory GHSA-rvv3-g6hj-g44x and therefore requires the narrow audit suppression and formal risk acceptance described there." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' == 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceDecisionRecorded)' == 'accepted-rpl-1.5'"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="AutoMapper licence: packing $(MSBuildProjectName) with the Reciprocal Public License 1.5 recorded as ACCEPTED for AutoMapper $(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion), from the committed record in '$(ErpLicenceDecisionRecordFile)'. Confirm PackageLicenseExpression '$(PackageLicenseExpression)' still states the intended posture before publishing. See docs/security/risk-register.md (RISK-001)." />
		<Message Condition="'$(ErpAutoMapperLicenceConflict)' != 'true' AND '$(ErpAutoMapperPinnedVersion)' != ''"
		         Importance="high"
		         Text="AutoMapper licence: no reciprocal-licence conflict detected for $(MSBuildProjectName) - AutoMapper resolves to a permissively licensed version, so no owner decision is required to pack." />
	</Target>

	<!-- NOTHING IS SUPPRESSED IN THIS FILE, and no advisory diagnostic may be. The only
	     NoWarn set anywhere here is the taint family above, for one project; no NU19xx
	     code is silenced in this file or in any project manifest, so a project's NoWarn
	     is either the SDK default or that family. Verify against the build rather than
	     by text search:
	         dotnet msbuild <project> -getItem:NuGetAuditSuppress
	         dotnet msbuild <project> -getProperty:NoWarn
	     The item list is empty for every project, and no project's NoWarn carries an
	     NU19xx code. Note that NoWarn is NOT uniform across projects, so quoting one
	     project's value as though it were global is itself an error.

	     Accepting an advisory instead of clearing it is a governed act, not an edit: it
	     requires a recorded, justified risk acceptance in docs/security/risk-register.md
	     naming the advisory, its severity, why the upgrade is declined and who accepted
	     the residual risk, and it must name ONE narrowly scoped diagnostic - never a
	     wildcard, and never a wholesale removal of the promotion above, either of which
	     would silence every advisory in the graph and defeat Gate 2. RISK-001 carries
	     the one open case and its reversal steps. -->

</Project>
